Only 20% to 25% of commissioned army officers are West Point

96
00:06:14,980 --> 00:06:16,820
graduates.

97
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:20,160
But those who have undergone the life altering experience

98
00:06:20,260 --> 00:06:24,600
share the bond of a unique and rich tradition,

99
00:06:24,700 --> 00:06:27,160
a legacy that connects some of the greatest names

100
00:06:27,260 --> 00:06:31,240
in the United States military and spans a time frame older

101
00:06:31,340 --> 00:06:32,440
than the nation itself.

102
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:38,080
In the middle of the 18th century,

103
00:06:38,180 --> 00:06:40,880
West Point was part of an almost inaccessible wilderness

104
00:06:40,881 --> 00:06:44,080
buried deep in the New York Highlands.

105
00:06:44,180 --> 00:06:45,880
But within 25 years, it would become

106
00:06:45,980 --> 00:06:48,380
the single most important piece of land on the continent.

107
00:06:51,820 --> 00:06:54,020
During the American War for Independence,

108
00:06:54,120 --> 00:06:56,100
the Hudson River was the physical divide

109
00:06:56,200 --> 00:06:58,700
between the cities of Boston and New York,

110
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:00,860
and all other major cities and croplands

111
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:04,040
to the south and west.

112
00:07:04,140 --> 00:07:06,240
Both the revolutionaries and the British

113
00:07:06,340 --> 00:07:08,040
recognized the extreme importance

114
00:07:08,140 --> 00:07:10,180
of controlling the waterway.

115
00:07:10,181 --> 00:07:12,720
It was essential from the beginning

116
00:07:12,820 --> 00:07:14,960
to be able to exchange men and material

117
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:16,980
between the two sections.

118
00:07:17,080 --> 00:07:19,720
Indeed, both sides recognized that if the British ever

119
00:07:19,820 --> 00:07:21,560
gained control of the Hudson River Valley

120
00:07:21,660 --> 00:07:23,860
and prevented that exchange of men and material

121
00:07:23,960 --> 00:07:27,160
between the two sections, the revolution would be ended.

122
00:07:27,260 --> 00:07:29,040
With the outbreak of hostilities,

123
00:07:29,140 --> 00:07:31,460
the Americans moved quickly to keep the Hudson River

124
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:35,200
from falling under British control.

125
00:07:35,300 --> 00:07:40,040
On May 25, 1775, just one month after the battles

126
00:07:40,041 --> 00:07:42,380
at Lexington and Concord.

127
00:07:42,480 --> 00:07:44,540
The Continental Congress issued a resolve

128
00:07:44,640 --> 00:07:47,080
to build a defensive position in the highlands

129
00:07:47,180 --> 00:07:50,280
above the Hudson River.

130
00:07:50,380 --> 00:07:53,180
Located high on a bluff overlooking a very narrow bend

131
00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:57,900
in the river, West Point was ideal.

132
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:00,900
Enemy ships trying to sail past gun emplacements

133
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:02,900
would have to navigate the twisting waterway

134
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:07,140
while being showered by cannon fire.

135
00:08:07,141 --> 00:08:11,140
Colonel Bernard Romans and 24 crewmen arrived on August 29,

136
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:16,240
1775 to begin construction on the proposed fort complex.

137
00:08:20,280 --> 00:08:24,500
Five block houses, barracks measuring 80 by 20 feet,

138
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:28,900
storehouses, and a guard room would support five batteries

139
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:34,500
armed with 61 stationery and 21 swivel guns, as well as a fort

140
00:08:34,501 --> 00:08:37,500
bastions and a magazine.

141
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:40,100
When we think of fortifications here in the American

142
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:45,040
Revolution, we have to think of earthen fortifications,

143
00:08:45,140 --> 00:08:48,640
temporary field fortifications, not something that you would

144
00:08:48,740 --> 00:08:53,060
see in Europe of stone and masonry, but by and large,

145
00:08:53,160 --> 00:08:57,660
simply built with rock and dirt.

146
00:08:57,760 --> 00:09:00,900
Unfortunately, Romans proved to be a poor engineer

147
00:09:00,901 --> 00:09:04,000
and was unable to complete the fortifications.

148
00:09:04,100 --> 00:09:07,440
He had also decided to build on what is now known as Constitution

149
00:09:07,540 --> 00:09:10,140
Island, across the river from West Point.

150
00:09:10,240 --> 00:09:12,840
Because the island had a relatively low elevation,

151
00:09:12,940 --> 00:09:16,640
the unfinished batteries would be easy targets for enemy guns.

152
00:09:16,740 --> 00:09:18,860
The fortification proved so ineffective

153
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:22,660
that it was overrun by the British in the fall of 1777.

154
00:09:22,760 --> 00:09:26,260
They didn't have to fire a single shot.

155
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:28,800
But the Americans would receive a second chance,

156
00:09:28,801 --> 00:09:32,200
as the British were forced to pull out and reconsolidate,

157
00:09:32,300 --> 00:09:36,000
thanks to the work of a famous war hero named Benedict Arnold,

158
00:09:36,100 --> 00:09:38,640
who helped force the surrender of General John Burgoyne

159
00:09:38,740 --> 00:09:41,740
at the Battle of Saratoga.

160
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:43,940
Capitalizing on his good fortune,

161
00:09:44,040 --> 00:09:46,960
General Washington ordered a brilliant Polish military

162
00:09:47,060 --> 00:09:50,460
engineer and revolutionary, Thaddeus Kosciuszko,

163
00:09:50,560 --> 00:09:56,060
to fortify West Point and ensure it never be taken again.

164
00:09:56,061 --> 00:09:59,260
Khrushchusko saw in West Point the future of military

165
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:01,260
strongholds.

166
00:10:01,360 --> 00:10:04,800
Not a single fort, but a fortified position.

167
00:10:04,900 --> 00:10:09,800
Up to 2,700 soldiers would man over 100 cannons and additional

168
00:10:09,900 --> 00:10:12,200
mortars to defend the river.

169
00:10:12,300 --> 00:10:15,320
Multiple gun emplacements were built in concentric rings to

170
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:19,720
provide fire support for each other, as well as protect two

171
00:10:19,820 --> 00:10:23,860
water-level strongholds, which would fire nearly point blank

172
00:10:23,861 --> 00:10:26,860
point blank on any ship trying to navigate the narrow and

173
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:30,100
treacherous S-curve in the river.

174
00:10:30,200 --> 00:10:32,660
If you were on a British ship sailing up the Hudson,

175
00:10:32,760 --> 00:10:36,600
approaching West Point, the first thing you do is you look

176
00:10:36,700 --> 00:10:41,080
up and you see imposing cliffs that dominate you, sheer rock

177
00:10:41,180 --> 00:10:44,840
walls, and bristling from all levels up

178
00:10:44,940 --> 00:10:47,480
above are artillery pieces.

179
00:10:47,580 --> 00:10:50,660
And at water's level, point blank,

180
00:10:50,760 --> 00:10:53,860
looking at the side of your ship, more artillery.

181
00:10:53,861 --> 00:10:57,200
So you would have been a very foolish ship captain,

182
00:10:57,300 --> 00:10:59,960
indeed, to have sailed into that.

183
00:11:00,060 --> 00:11:03,500
But the technological jewel in the crown of early West Point

184
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:07,440
may have been the 1,700-foot chain, which was strung

185
00:11:07,540 --> 00:11:12,320
across the Hudson River to stop any ship that tried to pass.

186
00:11:12,420 --> 00:11:14,580
At nearly 2 feet long and 1 and 1 1

187
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:18,520
half feet wide, the individual links fashioned by Sterling

188
00:11:18,620 --> 00:11:22,420
Iron Works would take over three months to forge.

189
00:11:22,421 --> 00:11:25,180
assembled at New Windsor, New York, about nine miles north

190
00:11:25,280 --> 00:11:28,560
of West Point, the chain was transported downriver

191
00:11:28,660 --> 00:11:33,760
and first installed on May 3, 1778.

192
00:11:33,860 --> 00:11:37,060
Floating upon 14 wooden rafts, the obstruction

193
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:39,880
was positioned where the river turned sharply.

194
00:11:39,980 --> 00:11:41,940
Ships trying to safely navigate the bend

195
00:11:42,040 --> 00:11:44,480
would have to come to a complete stop or risk

196
00:11:44,580 --> 00:11:47,880
grounding, leaving no momentum to break through the floating

197
00:11:47,980 --> 00:11:50,020
barrier.

198
00:11:50,021 --> 00:11:53,120
The weight of the chain has been variously estimated

199
00:11:53,220 --> 00:11:55,860
at between 80 and 150 tons.

200
00:11:55,960 --> 00:11:57,960
When Fortress West Point was completed,

201
00:11:58,060 --> 00:12:00,760
you had the chain, you had shore batteries, forts,

202
00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:04,500
and redoubts, making this the strongest defensive position

203
00:12:04,600 --> 00:12:06,340
on the Hudson River Valley.

204
00:12:06,440 --> 00:12:09,740
West Point was nearly impregnable from the outside.

205
00:12:09,840 --> 00:12:12,640
So much so, the British never again attempted to take it

206
00:12:12,740 --> 00:12:14,640
by force.

207
00:12:14,740 --> 00:12:17,640
But it was vulnerable, and its weakness

208
00:12:17,641 --> 00:12:20,940
would make it the backdrop for America's most infamous act of

209
00:12:21,040 --> 00:12:26,040
treason and lead to the downfall of one of the nation's greatest

210
00:12:26,140 --> 00:12:31,980
war heroes. The cost to put a cadet through four

211
00:12:32,080 --> 00:12:37,600
years at West Point is estimated at over $225,000 and is entirely

212
00:12:37,700 --> 00:12:45,740
funded by taxpayers. By 1779, the fortress at West

213
00:12:45,741 --> 00:12:48,140
stood as a technological triumph,

214
00:12:48,240 --> 00:12:51,140
ready to defend the strategically vital Hudson River

215
00:12:51,240 --> 00:12:53,780
and keep it from falling under British control.

216
00:12:53,880 --> 00:12:55,620
But cannons and fortifications would

217
00:12:55,720 --> 00:13:00,360
be unable to defend against West Point's greatest threat.

218
00:13:00,460 --> 00:13:03,020
Probably the most dramatic moment

219
00:13:03,120 --> 00:13:05,420
in the whole American Revolution,

220
00:13:05,520 --> 00:13:08,200
Washington put one of his best generals in command

221
00:13:08,300 --> 00:13:09,800
of West Point in 1780.

222
00:13:09,900 --> 00:13:11,840
His name was Benedict Arnold.

223
00:13:11,940 --> 00:13:14,940
But Arnold had grown very unhappy with the whole

224
00:13:14,941 --> 00:13:17,340
the whole revolution and with the Continental Congress

225
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:18,540
in particular.

226
00:13:18,640 --> 00:13:20,440
He felt they had been persecuting him.

227
00:13:20,540 --> 00:13:22,840
And so he tried to cut a deal with the British

228
00:13:22,940 --> 00:13:25,880
to surrender West Point to them.

229
00:13:25,980 --> 00:13:28,880
But all sorts of things went wrong.

230
00:13:28,980 --> 00:13:31,820
So important was the fort that the British high command

231
00:13:31,920 --> 00:13:34,320
dispatched their head of intelligence, Major John

232
00:13:34,420 --> 00:13:38,660
Andre, to secretly meet with Arnold.

233
00:13:38,760 --> 00:13:41,060
Poor Andre was caught on his way back to New York

234
00:13:41,061 --> 00:13:44,700
with the final arrangements to betray the fortress

235
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:46,460
and eventually hanged.

236
00:13:46,560 --> 00:13:50,200
And capturing Andre betrayed the whole plan,

237
00:13:50,300 --> 00:13:54,600
and West Point was saved, and the revolution, I might add.

238
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:57,180
Benedict Arnold would flee across enemy lines

239
00:13:57,280 --> 00:14:01,280
and be instated as a British officer.

240
00:14:01,380 --> 00:14:04,180
And though no shells had fallen on the fortress,

241
00:14:04,280 --> 00:14:07,320
the damage done by his treason left scars deeper

242
00:14:07,420 --> 00:14:08,720
than cannon fire ever could.

243
00:14:11,060 --> 00:14:14,260
actions would do little to allay an already deep-rooted

244
00:14:14,360 --> 00:14:16,760
distrust of the military amongst freedom-loving

245
00:14:16,860 --> 00:14:20,300
Americans. After the end of the Revolution

246
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:24,300
in 1783, the young nation would almost completely disband the

247
00:14:24,400 --> 00:14:28,280
army, a decision which in time would nearly destroy the

248
00:14:28,380 --> 00:14:33,380
country. In June of 1784, the United

249
00:14:33,480 --> 00:14:39,860
States of America was defended by 80 men and one warship.

250
00:14:39,861 --> 00:14:45,160
That was the entire defense establishment of the new United States.

251
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:49,480
The Americans basically distrusted the army.

252
00:14:50,140 --> 00:14:56,660
They were concerned that a standing army would actually be a threat to the new republic.

253
00:14:57,060 --> 00:15:02,340
The history of nations is that in times of turmoil, of turbulence, like a revolution,

254
00:15:02,880 --> 00:15:05,640
the military all too often tends to take over.

255
00:15:05,641 --> 00:15:08,940
So their answer was to not have a military.

256
00:15:09,860 --> 00:15:14,360
Instead, the United States would depend upon the militia, citizen

257
00:15:14,460 --> 00:15:18,260
soldiers who would rise up in times of need to defend the

258
00:15:18,360 --> 00:15:20,300
country. But the scarcely trained,

259
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:22,800
part-time soldiers would still need to be led by trained

260
00:15:22,900 --> 00:15:27,220
military professionals. Many former revolutionaries,

261
00:15:27,320 --> 00:15:30,820
including the first president, George Washington, saw the need

262
00:15:30,920 --> 00:15:33,920
for a military school which would instruct officers in the art

263
00:15:34,020 --> 00:15:38,920
of war and the sciences of artillery and fortification.

264
00:15:38,921 --> 00:15:42,520
A primary concern had been the American dependence on foreign

265
00:15:42,620 --> 00:15:46,520
military expertise during the revolution.

266
00:15:46,620 --> 00:15:50,460
George Washington, John Adams, Alexander Hamilton, Henry

267
00:15:50,560 --> 00:15:53,360
Knox, Baron von Steuben, and others suggested it would be

268
00:15:53,460 --> 00:15:56,980
an excellent idea to train Americans in these arts of war.

269
00:15:57,080 --> 00:15:58,980
When the proposal came up, however,

270
00:15:59,080 --> 00:16:02,040
in Washington's cabinet meeting in 1793,

271
00:16:02,140 --> 00:16:05,020
his Secretary of State, one Thomas Jefferson, said,

272
00:16:05,120 --> 00:16:06,180
you can't do it.

273
00:16:06,280 --> 00:16:07,720
It's unconstitutional.

274
00:16:07,721 --> 00:16:12,460
constitutional. Jefferson contended that nowhere

275
00:16:12,461 --> 00:16:17,960
in the Constitution was there a provision for a military academy.

276
00:16:17,961 --> 00:16:22,960
Ironically, when he assumed the presidency in 1801, one of his

277
00:16:22,961 --> 00:16:27,480
first acts would be to lobby Congress to create such a school.

278
00:16:27,481 --> 00:16:31,780
By 1802, Jefferson would succeed in pushing Congress to declare

279
00:16:31,781 --> 00:16:36,120
the fort at West Point home to the newly created United States

280
00:16:36,121 --> 00:16:37,211
military academy.

281
00:16:37,212 --> 00:16:42,600
Jefferson, who had fought this all of his life, who thought it was unconstitutional,

282
00:16:43,200 --> 00:16:50,160
but now one of his very first acts as president is to do what Washington wanted to do years and

283
00:16:50,161 --> 00:16:51,848
years before. Why?

284
00:16:51,849 --> 00:16:58,320
Well, the president swears to preserve, to protect, and to defend the

285
00:16:58,321 --> 00:16:59,182
Constitution.

286
00:16:59,183 --> 00:17:04,620
Now, being a man of high intellect, Jefferson understood you can't do any of those

287
00:17:04,621 --> 00:17:08,500
without a military. Jefferson also realized most

288
00:17:08,501 --> 00:17:13,340
American colleges at the time were readying graduates for the church, law, or medicine.

289
00:17:14,040 --> 00:17:19,700
Not one school in the decades-old country taught engineering, a skill the young, underdeveloped

290
00:17:19,701 --> 00:17:24,220
nation desperately needed. To Jefferson, a small military school

291
00:17:24,221 --> 00:17:29,880
far from the nation's capital, teaching basic mathematics and simple engineering was ideal.

292
00:17:29,881 --> 00:17:35,680
Only two students would graduate West Point the first year.

293
00:17:36,380 --> 00:17:38,760
And over Jefferson's entire eight-year presidency,

294
00:17:39,220 --> 00:17:42,100
only 50 would graduate to become commissioned officers.

295
00:17:43,300 --> 00:17:46,360
There were buildings left from the Revolution,

296
00:17:46,500 --> 00:17:47,900
a number of them still available.

297
00:17:48,540 --> 00:17:51,540
One of those was turned into an academy.

298
00:17:52,200 --> 00:17:55,900
It was, as we are told, about the size of a small country school.

299
00:17:55,901 --> 00:17:59,700
But that was certainly large enough for the dozen or so

300
00:17:59,800 --> 00:18:04,140
young men who would attend there.

301
00:18:04,240 --> 00:18:07,440
The school was poorly run and underfunded,

302
00:18:07,540 --> 00:18:10,840
partially due to fear of a well-organized military,

303
00:18:10,940 --> 00:18:13,420
partially because the men charged with running the school

304
00:18:13,520 --> 00:18:17,260
were commissioned engineers and spent most of their time

305
00:18:17,360 --> 00:18:21,660
traveling, building, and maintaining military forts.

306
00:18:21,760 --> 00:18:24,900
You reach the point, in fact, by 1812,

307
00:18:24,901 --> 00:18:28,800
that the superintendent and one newly arrived cadet constituted

308
00:18:28,900 --> 00:18:32,540
the military academy that had gotten that bad.

309
00:18:32,640 --> 00:18:35,240
It would take a national tragedy for West Point

310
00:18:35,340 --> 00:18:38,140
to become a serious institution.

311
00:18:38,240 --> 00:18:41,140
The War of 1812 was a military disaster

312
00:18:41,240 --> 00:18:43,360
for the United States of America.

313
00:18:43,460 --> 00:18:45,660
The young country would see the British once again

314
00:18:45,760 --> 00:18:48,360
on American soil.

315
00:18:48,460 --> 00:18:50,660
The untrained militia proved no match

316
00:18:50,760 --> 00:18:52,560
for the regular British Army.

317
00:18:52,561 --> 00:18:55,560
Before the end of hostilities in 1814,

318
00:18:55,561 --> 00:18:57,560
the United States would watch in horror

319
00:18:57,561 --> 00:19:00,560
as its enemy marched to the nation's capital

320
00:19:00,561 --> 00:19:03,560
and set fire to the very symbol of American freedom,

321
00:19:03,561 --> 00:19:05,560
the White House.

322
00:19:05,561 --> 00:19:08,560
Meanwhile, the West Pointers, who were in the army,

323
00:19:08,561 --> 00:19:12,560
performed very well, particularly in their specialty.

324
00:19:12,561 --> 00:19:15,560
Not one fort that was designed by a West Pointer

325
00:19:15,561 --> 00:19:17,560
in the War of 1812 was captured by the enemy.

326
00:19:17,561 --> 00:19:21,560
A number of West Pointers performed brilliantly,

327
00:19:21,561 --> 00:19:23,120
leading troops in battle.

328
00:19:23,220 --> 00:19:27,960
So West Point came out of this war looking very good

329
00:19:28,060 --> 00:19:31,100
and had to be taken seriously from now on.

330
00:19:31,200 --> 00:19:34,300
In hopes of bettering the academy, in 1812,

331
00:19:34,400 --> 00:19:38,820
Congress passed a bill raising the entrance requirements.

332
00:19:38,920 --> 00:19:43,420
All cadets would now be between the ages of 14 and 21

333
00:19:43,520 --> 00:19:47,160
and well-versed in reading, writing, and arithmetic.

334
00:19:47,260 --> 00:19:49,960
While higher than before, at the time,

335
00:19:49,961 --> 00:19:52,460
These standards were considered inferior to other American

336
00:19:52,560 --> 00:19:55,700
colleges, where previous studies in Latin and Greek

337
00:19:55,800 --> 00:19:57,600
were expected.

338
00:19:57,700 --> 00:20:00,100
But the Academy's goal was to serve the Republic

339
00:20:00,200 --> 00:20:02,600
by taking young men from around the country who

340
00:20:02,700 --> 00:20:05,700
were loyal to their states and the voting public,

341
00:20:05,800 --> 00:20:10,820
and transform them into capable army officers.

342
00:20:10,920 --> 00:20:14,760
West Pointer's relative success during the War of 1812

343
00:20:14,860 --> 00:20:16,520
would also lead to additional funding

344
00:20:16,620 --> 00:20:18,820
from a grateful Congress.

345
00:20:18,821 --> 00:20:22,720
A few years later, they built real barracks for the cadets.

346
00:20:22,820 --> 00:20:26,420
The first two barracks, South Barracks and North Barracks,

347
00:20:26,520 --> 00:20:31,660
were built in the middle of the second decade in the 1815 to

348
00:20:31,760 --> 00:20:34,560
1817 time frame.

349
00:20:34,660 --> 00:20:36,180
But the greatest strides for the school

350
00:20:36,280 --> 00:20:38,280
would be made on the academic front,

351
00:20:38,380 --> 00:20:43,020
under the leadership of a former graduate and teacher,

352
00:20:43,120 --> 00:20:45,720
Captain Sylvanus Thayer, who was appointed

353
00:20:45,721 --> 00:20:50,520
superintendent in 1817. Thayer stressed daily recitation

354
00:20:50,620 --> 00:20:55,160
of the lessons, forcing students to study every night.

355
00:20:55,260 --> 00:20:58,160
Each cadet was also ranked with his peers.

356
00:20:58,260 --> 00:21:01,240
The ratings were posted every month, forcing competition

357
00:21:01,340 --> 00:21:03,700
amongst the students.

358
00:21:03,800 --> 00:21:06,400
Additionally, the best students in each subject

359
00:21:06,500 --> 00:21:09,940
were put into the same classes, which created an accelerated

360
00:21:10,040 --> 00:21:13,220
learning program. But perhaps Thayer's greatest

361
00:21:13,221 --> 00:21:16,460
was to increase the quality of the education

362
00:21:16,461 --> 00:21:19,220
by finding better teachers.

363
00:21:19,221 --> 00:21:23,220
Luckily, the best place to find well-educated military engineers

364
00:21:23,221 --> 00:21:25,220
was West Point.

365
00:21:25,221 --> 00:21:28,220
And when a man graduated and did very well

366
00:21:28,221 --> 00:21:30,220
while he was going through West Point,

367
00:21:30,221 --> 00:21:33,220
when he graduated, Thayer would tap him to come back

368
00:21:33,221 --> 00:21:37,220
and teach at the school as soon as he could get his mitts on him.

369
00:21:37,221 --> 00:21:39,220
And sometimes they didn't even go away.

370
00:21:39,221 --> 00:21:40,220
He kept them.

371
00:21:40,221 --> 00:21:47,220
And so he was always looking to get the best talent for the school in every way he could think of.

372
00:21:47,221 --> 00:21:54,220
I think as individuals look over Thayer's superintendency and the fact that he was here 16 years

373
00:21:54,221 --> 00:22:00,220
and trained so many individuals who were so important in the academy as well as the nation's development,

374
00:22:00,221 --> 00:22:05,220
that Thayer received the recognition as being called the father of the military academy.

375
00:22:05,221 --> 00:22:08,620
In the minds of many, the selection of the Academy's

376
00:22:08,720 --> 00:22:12,260
motto in 1898, duty, honor, country,

377
00:22:12,360 --> 00:22:14,560
was merely the institutionalization

378
00:22:14,660 --> 00:22:17,960
of Thayer's ideals into words.

379
00:22:18,060 --> 00:22:20,960
Thayer's West Point graduates would be tested in battle

380
00:22:21,060 --> 00:22:23,680
during frontier skirmishes with Native Americans

381
00:22:23,780 --> 00:22:27,980
during the 1820s and 30s, as well as the Mexican-American

382
00:22:28,080 --> 00:22:32,280
War in 1846.

383
00:22:32,281 --> 00:22:36,220
At the end of the war, Winfield Scott gave a toast at a banquet

384
00:22:36,320 --> 00:22:38,620
in Mexico City after the city surrendered.

385
00:22:38,720 --> 00:22:42,920
He said to West Point, but for its science,

386
00:22:43,020 --> 00:22:46,020
we could never have won this war.

387
00:22:46,120 --> 00:22:50,640
In 1843, Congress passed legislation requiring one cadet

388
00:22:50,740 --> 00:22:52,940
be admitted from each congressional district,

389
00:22:53,040 --> 00:22:57,340
making into law a tradition that had flourished for years.

390
00:22:57,440 --> 00:22:59,780
Still, the classes remained small.

391
00:22:59,781 --> 00:23:04,780
That same year, only 39 cadets would graduate.

392
00:23:04,880 --> 00:23:08,020
But these graduates, having completed their studies in math,

393
00:23:08,120 --> 00:23:12,020
French, chemistry, drawing, and natural philosophy, today known

394
00:23:12,120 --> 00:23:16,540
as physics, would have a truly profound effect on America.

395
00:23:16,640 --> 00:23:19,740
West Point's greatest benefit to the country would come in the

396
00:23:19,840 --> 00:23:23,140
form of civil engineering feats.

397
00:23:23,240 --> 00:23:26,280
Many graduates were involved with the design and engineering

398
00:23:26,380 --> 00:23:29,780
of nationwide railroad and telegraph systems.

399
00:23:29,781 --> 00:23:33,580
as well as building harbors and lighthouses to open American

400
00:23:33,680 --> 00:23:35,180
waterways.

401
00:23:35,280 --> 00:23:38,180
But soon, West Pointers would be called on for their skills

402
00:23:38,280 --> 00:23:41,820
in warfare, as the country they helped link with bridges, roads,

403
00:23:41,920 --> 00:23:45,820
and rail would begin to tear itself apart.

404
00:23:45,920 --> 00:23:49,600
The first cadets to attend West Point in 1802 were paid $16

405
00:23:49,700 --> 00:23:52,740
per month and received two meal rations per day.

406
00:23:56,740 --> 00:23:58,980
Described as a battle between brothers,

407
00:23:58,981 --> 00:24:01,980
the American Civil War would seem even more intimate

408
00:24:02,080 --> 00:24:04,980
for West Point graduates.

409
00:24:05,080 --> 00:24:08,420
Nearly 90% of the top military leaders on both sides,

410
00:24:08,520 --> 00:24:12,820
including Lee, Grant, Sherman, and even Jefferson Davis

411
00:24:12,920 --> 00:24:17,140
himself, had graduated from the US Military Academy.

412
00:24:17,240 --> 00:24:20,640
Of the 60 most important battles of the Civil War,

413
00:24:20,740 --> 00:24:24,940
55 were commanded on both sides by West Pointers.

414
00:24:25,040 --> 00:24:28,980
I guess the key idea that was taught to West Point

415
00:24:28,981 --> 00:24:34,280
pointers was celerity, which is a Latin-like term for speed.

416
00:24:34,281 --> 00:24:38,220
If you wanted to get anywhere, you had to move fast.

417
00:24:38,221 --> 00:24:42,020
If you attacked, it had to be done with maximum impact

418
00:24:42,021 --> 00:24:43,760
and maximum speed.

419
00:24:43,761 --> 00:24:48,160
They also understood principles like concentration of force.

420
00:24:48,161 --> 00:24:51,440
You didn't attack somebody six different places.

421
00:24:51,441 --> 00:24:53,380
You concentrated all the men you had,

422
00:24:53,381 --> 00:24:56,100
and you hit them at its weakest point.

423
00:24:56,101 --> 00:25:00,400
Colleague versus colleague, friend versus friend, brother

424
00:25:00,401 --> 00:25:02,540
versus brother.

425
00:25:02,541 --> 00:25:05,440
The West Pointers would use their deadly classroom lessons

426
00:25:05,441 --> 00:25:09,340
against each other with the horrible honor, duty, and courage

427
00:25:09,341 --> 00:25:11,740
expected of an academy-trained leader.

428
00:25:14,660 --> 00:25:18,980
With the end of the war in 1865, the academy would rely on new

429
00:25:18,981 --> 00:25:22,600
incoming cadets to help heal the wounds left by the conflict.

430
00:25:26,100 --> 00:25:30,000
Since 1843, the student body of the United States Military

431
00:25:30,001 --> 00:25:33,540
Academy at West Point has only allowed entrance to the school

432
00:25:33,541 --> 00:25:36,840
via presidential and congressional appointments.

433
00:25:36,841 --> 00:25:38,580
To lawmakers, this seemed the best way

434
00:25:38,581 --> 00:25:41,800
to create an officer corps that was as diverse as the states

435
00:25:41,801 --> 00:25:46,260
they represented and loyal to the voting public.

436
00:25:46,261 --> 00:25:49,260
The increase in statehood at the end of the 19th and early 20th

437
00:25:49,261 --> 00:25:54,240
centuries caused the student body to expand.

438
00:25:54,241 --> 00:25:57,940
By 1900, 381 students were in attendance,

439
00:25:57,941 --> 00:26:01,040
and additional housing and educational facilities needed

440
00:26:01,041 --> 00:26:04,140
to be built to keep pace with the burgeoning corps of cadets.

441
00:26:07,380 --> 00:26:12,040
After the turn of the century, the academy was again expanding

442
00:26:12,041 --> 00:26:16,660
and received money from Congress for the construction of new buildings.

443
00:26:17,800 --> 00:26:22,400
By this point, we had been rapidly converting from wood to granite

444
00:26:22,401 --> 00:26:25,100
and build a lot of the barracks facilities

445
00:26:25,101 --> 00:26:28,900
and academic buildings during that time.

446
00:26:28,901 --> 00:26:32,740
In 1902, Congress appropriated $6 million

447
00:26:32,741 --> 00:26:35,140
for the new construction.

448
00:26:35,141 --> 00:26:38,340
The renowned Boston architectural firm of Cram, Goodhue,

449
00:26:38,341 --> 00:26:40,560
and Ferguson would win the contract

450
00:26:40,561 --> 00:26:42,760
with their stunning American Gothic design.

451
00:26:45,860 --> 00:26:50,600
They embraced what seemed to them to be the right architecture

452
00:26:50,601 --> 00:26:51,360
for the school.

453
00:26:51,361 --> 00:26:55,840
It required a Gothic fortress look, which I think is really

454
00:26:55,940 --> 00:26:57,340
quite suitable for the school.

455
00:26:57,440 --> 00:27:00,600
And it gives it a warlike atmosphere.

456
00:27:00,700 --> 00:27:03,840
And it also gives you that feeling of order

457
00:27:03,940 --> 00:27:07,140
that is very important to an army.

458
00:27:07,240 --> 00:27:11,040
By 1915, five new buildings had been constructed,

459
00:27:11,140 --> 00:27:14,780
including the Administration Building, East Academic

460
00:27:14,880 --> 00:27:18,580
Building, Riding Hall, North Barracks, and one of the most

461
00:27:18,581 --> 00:27:21,820
and identifiable landmarks to ever grace the campus,

462
00:27:21,920 --> 00:27:23,320
the chapel.

463
00:27:23,420 --> 00:27:25,620
Unfortunately, many of the older, obsolete buildings

464
00:27:25,720 --> 00:27:28,520
needed to be removed to make room for the new castle-like

465
00:27:28,620 --> 00:27:31,220
structures.

466
00:27:31,320 --> 00:27:35,340
One of the most beloved was the old chapel.

467
00:27:35,440 --> 00:27:37,940
The alumni got rather irate.

468
00:27:38,040 --> 00:27:42,680
Here, this place where they had worshipped from 1836 to 1910

469
00:27:42,780 --> 00:27:44,740
was going to be torn down.

470
00:27:44,741 --> 00:27:49,440
So they were able to get the structure disassembled and

471
00:27:49,540 --> 00:27:53,340
re-established, put together brick by brick at the site of

472
00:27:53,440 --> 00:27:55,680
the West Point Cemetery, where it stands today.

473
00:27:59,280 --> 00:28:01,900
But the school would soon undergo an academic change

474
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:04,800
to match its physical change.

475
00:28:04,900 --> 00:28:07,900
Though a victory for the United States, the Spanish-American

476
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:13,800
War of 1898 would point out inadequacies in Army leadership.

477
00:28:13,801 --> 00:28:16,440
Military experts complained the tactics utilized

478
00:28:16,540 --> 00:28:20,240
were little advanced from the days of the Civil War.

479
00:28:20,340 --> 00:28:22,640
The army needed to modernize its strategies

480
00:28:22,740 --> 00:28:26,540
and further educate its commanding officers.

481
00:28:26,640 --> 00:28:28,040
The solution?

482
00:28:28,140 --> 00:28:32,460
Create additional, more advanced schools.

483
00:28:32,560 --> 00:28:36,960
So West Point then fit into that array of schools

484
00:28:37,060 --> 00:28:40,200
by being the very first, the one that gave people

485
00:28:40,300 --> 00:28:42,760
their foundation of leadership, their foundation

486
00:28:42,761 --> 00:28:47,760
their foundation of character, their foundation in basic military training.

487
00:28:47,761 --> 00:28:54,760
Then, after West Point, they would go to a branch school at some point.

488
00:28:54,761 --> 00:28:59,760
Then the Command and General Staff College at Fort Leavenworth for mid-grade officers

489
00:28:59,761 --> 00:29:05,760
would take a year or sometimes two years, and they would study, ending up with a war college.

490
00:29:05,761 --> 00:29:09,760
So for the first time, as we begin the 20th century,

491
00:29:09,761 --> 00:29:13,360
you have the entire array of schools.

492
00:29:13,460 --> 00:29:15,800
The next major conflict for American forces

493
00:29:15,900 --> 00:29:18,860
would be on the battlefields of Europe during World War I.

494
00:29:21,500 --> 00:29:24,640
The sudden need to fill the ranks with capable officers

495
00:29:24,740 --> 00:29:28,740
caused West Point to graduate cadets in as little as one year.

496
00:29:28,840 --> 00:29:31,680
Though the cadets' classroom studies may have been lacking,

497
00:29:31,780 --> 00:29:35,660
West Point's entire purpose was to create military leaders,

498
00:29:35,760 --> 00:29:39,260
a task that involved more than studying old battles or equations

499
00:29:39,261 --> 00:29:41,300
in textbooks.

500
00:29:41,400 --> 00:29:44,200
Self-discipline, motivation, and honorable behavior

501
00:29:44,300 --> 00:29:46,560
were taught by example and flourished

502
00:29:46,660 --> 00:29:49,700
in the strict military atmosphere where senior cadets

503
00:29:49,800 --> 00:29:53,400
would organize and assign duties to lower classmen, who,

504
00:29:53,500 --> 00:29:56,020
in turn, would learn by following,

505
00:29:56,120 --> 00:29:58,920
gaining the confidence to lead and look after their own

506
00:29:59,020 --> 00:30:00,320
subordinates.

507
00:30:00,420 --> 00:30:03,720
These lessons would serve former students well, like class of

508
00:30:03,820 --> 00:30:07,660
1886 graduate John Blackjack Pershing, leader of the American

509
00:30:07,661 --> 00:30:11,740
American forces in World War I.

510
00:30:11,741 --> 00:30:14,760
Pershing was the perfect example of how much West Pointers

511
00:30:14,761 --> 00:30:16,620
were trained to care about their men.

512
00:30:16,621 --> 00:30:18,500
When he inspected a regiment or a division,

513
00:30:18,501 --> 00:30:22,160
he climbed up into the haylofts to see how the men were bedded

514
00:30:22,161 --> 00:30:23,100
down.

515
00:30:23,101 --> 00:30:25,740
And he went into the kitchens and sniffed the soup

516
00:30:25,741 --> 00:30:27,460
to make sure it was edible.

517
00:30:27,461 --> 00:30:30,340
He really wanted to see his men to make sure

518
00:30:30,341 --> 00:30:32,900
their physical side of their army life

519
00:30:32,901 --> 00:30:37,020
was done in the best possible fashion.

520
00:30:37,021 --> 00:30:40,860
After the war, a young upstart commander and class of 1903

521
00:30:40,861 --> 00:30:43,460
graduate by the name of Douglas MacArthur

522
00:30:43,461 --> 00:30:45,120
became superintendent at West Point.

523
00:30:48,040 --> 00:30:51,680
Having seen firsthand the rigors of modern warfare,

524
00:30:51,681 --> 00:30:57,100
MacArthur made physical fitness a major part of academy life.

525
00:30:57,101 --> 00:30:59,540
Gymnastics, boxing, and intramural sports

526
00:30:59,541 --> 00:31:02,760
were added to the students' regimen.

527
00:31:02,761 --> 00:31:05,480
A good soldier was a healthy soldier.

528
00:31:05,481 --> 00:31:09,320
And the mandate, every cadet and athlete, was taken to heart.

529
00:31:11,620 --> 00:31:14,440
But with America's interests growing beyond its shores,

530
00:31:15,080 --> 00:31:18,040
the biggest changes for the United States Military Academy

531
00:31:18,041 --> 00:31:19,860
would not happen on the playing fields,

532
00:31:20,280 --> 00:31:21,660
but in the classrooms.

533
00:31:23,160 --> 00:31:25,100
The role of the Army had changed,

534
00:31:25,240 --> 00:31:28,640
and the role of the Academy began to change with it.

535
00:31:28,940 --> 00:31:30,520
More language was being taught.

536
00:31:30,940 --> 00:31:32,720
An English department was created.

537
00:31:33,240 --> 00:31:35,280
Literature was introduced in a real way.

538
00:31:35,480 --> 00:31:46,760
There was also a movement afoot, in fact, by the English professor Holt, to create a department of geography and history,

539
00:31:46,761 --> 00:31:55,820
a department that would broaden the education of the officers and give them more knowledge about parts of the world that they were going to be involved in.

540
00:31:57,020 --> 00:32:02,160
The young leaders from West Point would now be better educated than at any time in history.

541
00:32:05,480 --> 00:32:10,360
By 1930, the student body had swelled to 1,200 cadets,

542
00:32:10,361 --> 00:32:14,780
and West Point was feeling its growing pains.

543
00:32:14,781 --> 00:32:17,160
As the academy moves into the 1930s,

544
00:32:17,161 --> 00:32:19,860
the size of the academy and the number of cadets grows.

545
00:32:19,861 --> 00:32:22,600
So does the need for more facilities, more classrooms,

546
00:32:22,601 --> 00:32:23,660
more barracks.

547
00:32:23,661 --> 00:32:26,940
And those are added somewhat in a haphazard manner,

548
00:32:26,941 --> 00:32:31,640
no coordinated plan of expanding the academy.

549
00:32:31,641 --> 00:32:35,480
And that's understandable with the approach of World War II.

550
00:32:35,481 --> 00:32:38,880
The prospect of another global conflict forced the military

551
00:32:38,980 --> 00:32:42,420
and West Point into high gear.

552
00:32:42,520 --> 00:32:45,220
There had been a problem in the 1930s that there was

553
00:32:45,320 --> 00:32:48,220
insufficient training area here for some of the military

554
00:32:48,320 --> 00:32:49,720
skills.

555
00:32:49,820 --> 00:32:53,060
And so in the late 1930s, early 1940s, the government

556
00:32:53,160 --> 00:32:55,940
purchased additional territory to the south and west to

557
00:32:56,040 --> 00:33:00,280
enable us to provide a diversified and very demanding

558
00:33:00,380 --> 00:33:05,480
military training program at site here at West Point.

559
00:33:05,481 --> 00:33:08,280
The additional acreage would allow cadets to experience

560
00:33:08,380 --> 00:33:11,780
more realistic combat training while providing more room

561
00:33:11,880 --> 00:33:15,820
for the traditional artillery and cannon practice.

562
00:33:15,920 --> 00:33:17,960
The combination of a broader education

563
00:33:18,060 --> 00:33:19,660
and increased practical training

564
00:33:19,760 --> 00:33:22,060
would help many graduates who would soon find themselves

565
00:33:22,160 --> 00:33:27,500
leading American troops into the greatest armed conflict

566
00:33:27,600 --> 00:33:28,300
in history.

567
00:33:31,780 --> 00:33:35,480
In 1877, Henry Ossian Flipper became

568
00:33:35,481 --> 00:33:38,480
African American to graduate from the US Military Academy

569
00:33:38,580 --> 00:33:39,480
at West Point.

570
00:33:43,480 --> 00:33:47,320
World War II would embroil the United States of America in yet

571
00:33:47,420 --> 00:33:49,420
another global conflict.

572
00:33:49,520 --> 00:33:52,540
And the fate of the nation would rest squarely on former West

573
00:33:52,640 --> 00:33:55,140
Point graduates.

574
00:33:55,240 --> 00:33:59,540
Generals with names like Eisenhower and Bradley, Patton,

575
00:33:59,640 --> 00:34:02,840
and MacArthur would lead the country through the most brutal

576
00:34:02,841 --> 00:34:06,080
brutal conflict ever seen by humankind.

577
00:34:06,180 --> 00:34:09,640
And indeed, the class of 1915 is called the class

578
00:34:09,740 --> 00:34:13,580
the stars fell on, because of the 164 graduates,

579
00:34:13,680 --> 00:34:15,880
59 became generals.

580
00:34:15,980 --> 00:34:19,020
Eisenhower and Omar Bradley are among the two more famous

581
00:34:19,120 --> 00:34:21,560
generals coming out of that class.

582
00:34:21,660 --> 00:34:24,600
But West Point's role was changing.

583
00:34:24,700 --> 00:34:28,260
Never again would US Military Academy graduates so dominate

584
00:34:28,360 --> 00:34:32,040
the officer corps during wartime.

585
00:34:32,041 --> 00:34:35,540
1946 was the first year that more officers graduated from

586
00:34:35,640 --> 00:34:39,940
the College Reserve Officer Training Corps, known as ROTC,

587
00:34:40,040 --> 00:34:42,980
than from West Point.

588
00:34:43,080 --> 00:34:45,680
Changing world politics would continue to increase the need

589
00:34:45,780 --> 00:34:49,000
for educated officers.

590
00:34:49,100 --> 00:34:53,000
What we had always done in every war throughout our history

591
00:34:53,100 --> 00:34:55,540
is to build up the Army when it started,

592
00:34:55,640 --> 00:35:00,300
and at the end of it, destroy it, disband it.

593
00:35:00,301 --> 00:35:01,200
And we started.

594
00:35:01,300 --> 00:35:04,300
We began to do that in the first year or two

595
00:35:04,400 --> 00:35:05,800
after World War II.

596
00:35:05,900 --> 00:35:08,600
Then the Cold War started.

597
00:35:08,700 --> 00:35:11,440
Tensions between America and their one-time ally,

598
00:35:11,540 --> 00:35:13,540
the Soviet Union, would forever change

599
00:35:13,640 --> 00:35:15,840
the political landscape.

600
00:35:15,940 --> 00:35:19,660
All branches of the US military would stand vigilant, ready

601
00:35:19,760 --> 00:35:21,560
to defend American interests wherever

602
00:35:21,660 --> 00:35:24,100
the threat of communism appeared.

603
00:35:24,200 --> 00:35:27,960
But at West Point, changes would be slow in coming.

604
00:35:28,060 --> 00:35:30,060
In the 50s and 60s now, and here

605
00:35:30,061 --> 00:35:34,400
Here I know firsthand I was a cadet from 52 to 56.

606
00:35:34,401 --> 00:35:38,500
When I entered in 1952, we had a curriculum that was really

607
00:35:38,501 --> 00:35:40,640
pre-World War II.

608
00:35:40,641 --> 00:35:43,440
We picked up nuclear physics, and we

609
00:35:43,441 --> 00:35:46,880
had modern communications in electronics and so forth.

610
00:35:46,881 --> 00:35:52,020
But the curriculum, while within courses and within programs,

611
00:35:52,021 --> 00:35:53,520
there were evolutionary changes.

612
00:35:53,521 --> 00:35:56,120
The curriculum itself was the same.

613
00:35:56,121 --> 00:35:59,080
But that would soon change.

614
00:35:59,081 --> 00:36:04,360
numerous all-too-real conflicts would punctuate the Cold War.

615
00:36:04,361 --> 00:36:07,820
Limited fighting in Korea and the jungles of Vietnam

616
00:36:07,821 --> 00:36:10,160
would create a new kind of warfare,

617
00:36:10,161 --> 00:36:13,320
and West Point would produce a new kind of officer.

618
00:36:13,321 --> 00:36:15,600
Your beachhead is just your initial objective.

619
00:36:15,601 --> 00:36:18,160
I think that's not a very practical way to attack a beach.

620
00:36:18,161 --> 00:36:21,240
Over the years, the art of war changed,

621
00:36:21,241 --> 00:36:24,900
as an officer could no longer stand in one place, for instance,

622
00:36:24,901 --> 00:36:26,200
and see all of his command.

623
00:36:26,201 --> 00:36:34,380
So the demands on an officer changed from the certainty that goes with training to being able to deal with uncertainty.

624
00:36:34,381 --> 00:36:40,160
And the way you deal with uncertainty is you build an officer corps that's educated as well as trained.

625
00:36:40,780 --> 00:36:49,540
Because an educated officer is an officer who is taught not what to think, but how to think.

626
00:36:51,640 --> 00:36:54,480
West Point once again altered its coursework,

627
00:36:54,481 --> 00:36:57,180
creating an even broader curriculum.

628
00:36:57,280 --> 00:36:59,560
For the military, the best way to find solutions

629
00:36:59,660 --> 00:37:02,260
to complicated problems was to have leaders

630
00:37:02,360 --> 00:37:04,560
with extensive knowledge in many areas,

631
00:37:04,660 --> 00:37:09,600
such as politics, history, and even business.

632
00:37:09,700 --> 00:37:11,600
Business is a lot like war.

633
00:37:11,700 --> 00:37:13,700
I mean, you have an enemy, you have a competitor,

634
00:37:13,800 --> 00:37:16,140
and you deal with uncertainty, you deal with the unknown,

635
00:37:16,240 --> 00:37:18,140
and you're expected to perform.

636
00:37:18,240 --> 00:37:21,800
You've got a mission to perform to a series of standards,

637
00:37:21,801 --> 00:37:26,800
and whether it's winning in battle or whether it's building

638
00:37:26,900 --> 00:37:29,500
a corporation that's profitable, in many ways,

639
00:37:29,600 --> 00:37:31,940
the same skills apply.

640
00:37:32,040 --> 00:37:35,440
The 1950s and 60s would see another huge increase

641
00:37:35,540 --> 00:37:39,260
in the number of cadets studying at West Point.

642
00:37:39,360 --> 00:37:43,260
Over 4,400 students would push the limits of the campus

643
00:37:43,360 --> 00:37:46,260
and lead to construction of new barracks and academic

644
00:37:46,360 --> 00:37:50,700
buildings, including a mess hall capable of simultaneously

645
00:37:50,701 --> 00:37:53,700
feeding the entire student body.

646
00:37:53,701 --> 00:37:57,700
But acquiring funding for the expansion had been a difficult task.

647
00:37:57,701 --> 00:38:00,700
When General Westmoreland was the superintendent,

648
00:38:00,701 --> 00:38:04,700
Kennedy was the president, he came to the Army-Navy game.

649
00:38:04,701 --> 00:38:07,700
In the second half, Kennedy turns to Westmoreland and says,

650
00:38:07,701 --> 00:38:10,700
General, why is it that the Naval Academy has turned out

651
00:38:10,701 --> 00:38:13,700
more students for the game than has West Point?

652
00:38:13,701 --> 00:38:16,700
And of course, anyone who knows the two schools knows that

653
00:38:16,701 --> 00:38:18,700
every student goes to that game.

654
00:38:18,701 --> 00:38:20,660
It wasn't a question of who turned out more.

655
00:38:21,620 --> 00:38:25,020
And General Westmoreland said, Mr. President, I'm glad you asked me that.

656
00:38:25,620 --> 00:38:29,220
So the second half of a very bad game for Army

657
00:38:29,221 --> 00:38:31,960
was spent with Westmoreland talking to the president

658
00:38:31,961 --> 00:38:33,960
about the need for more cadets.

659
00:38:34,600 --> 00:38:38,120
The increased student body and subsequent building boom

660
00:38:38,121 --> 00:38:41,060
had drastically changed the military academy at West Point.

661
00:38:42,220 --> 00:38:45,140
Still, many aspects of cadet life remained the same.

662
00:38:46,360 --> 00:38:48,100
Living conditions are much better than they were.

663
00:38:48,101 --> 00:38:50,560
The rooms were cool in the summer and warm in the winter.

664
00:38:50,561 --> 00:38:53,680
That certainly was not true of either season before.

665
00:38:53,681 --> 00:38:57,220
So that life is a little easier than it used to be.

666
00:38:57,221 --> 00:39:00,320
But there are many things that are the same.

667
00:39:00,321 --> 00:39:01,960
Everyone still wears a uniform.

668
00:39:01,961 --> 00:39:04,000
Everyone goes to class.

669
00:39:04,001 --> 00:39:07,960
Class sizes are small, but classes are still very much

670
00:39:07,961 --> 00:39:09,880
the way they were, have always been.

671
00:39:09,881 --> 00:39:13,760
So cadet life changes with the times.

672
00:39:13,761 --> 00:39:16,380
But there's probably more that's familiar than is

673
00:39:16,381 --> 00:39:19,380
from decade to decade.

674
00:39:19,480 --> 00:39:22,380
But the familiar comfort and traditions of West Point

675
00:39:22,480 --> 00:39:25,820
would offer little protection, as the US military found

676
00:39:25,920 --> 00:39:29,220
itself mired in Vietnam and distrusted by the American

677
00:39:29,320 --> 00:39:30,420
public.

678
00:39:30,520 --> 00:39:33,360
At the end of the conflict, the Army and West Point were

679
00:39:33,460 --> 00:39:37,040
in disarray and would need to spend the next two decades

680
00:39:37,140 --> 00:39:38,040
rebuilding.

681
00:39:41,040 --> 00:39:45,340
With 18,701 pipes, the Chapel organ at West Point is the

682
00:39:45,341 --> 00:39:47,340
is the world's largest church pipe organ.

683
00:39:51,340 --> 00:39:55,340
In the early 1970s, as the unpopular war in Vietnam

684
00:39:55,440 --> 00:39:58,680
came to an end, troops returning from combat

685
00:39:58,780 --> 00:40:03,700
would face a new enemy, the American people.

686
00:40:03,800 --> 00:40:06,700
The 1970s was a difficult time for the Army

687
00:40:06,800 --> 00:40:08,200
and for West Point.

688
00:40:08,300 --> 00:40:10,700
You had the end of the Vietnam War,

689
00:40:10,800 --> 00:40:14,200
where there was a lot of criticism of the military service.

690
00:40:14,201 --> 00:40:20,440
service and indeed of the military academy.

691
00:40:20,540 --> 00:40:23,940
But this hostile environment would force change and allow

692
00:40:24,040 --> 00:40:27,640
West Point to become one of the finest military schools the world

693
00:40:27,740 --> 00:40:30,960
has ever seen.

694
00:40:31,060 --> 00:40:34,960
For over 170 years at West Point, not one woman had been allowed

695
00:40:35,060 --> 00:40:36,960
admission to the school.

696
00:40:37,060 --> 00:40:42,260
A 1975 act of Congress would rectify that problem.

697
00:40:42,261 --> 00:40:46,060
In 1976, the first female cadets would enter the Army,

698
00:40:46,160 --> 00:40:49,660
Navy, and Air Force academies.

699
00:40:49,760 --> 00:40:52,400
With great difficulty, West Point would open its doors

700
00:40:52,500 --> 00:40:55,400
upon this new era.

701
00:40:55,500 --> 00:40:58,700
The first women who entered, my hat's off to them,

702
00:40:58,800 --> 00:41:01,520
because fewer than half were able to graduate.

703
00:41:01,620 --> 00:41:06,820
It was not an inviting place if you were a young woman

704
00:41:06,920 --> 00:41:08,460
entering as a plea.

705
00:41:08,560 --> 00:41:11,220
There were 100 and some in that first class,

706
00:41:11,221 --> 00:41:16,220
But when they looked around at the 4,500 other cadets,

707
00:41:16,320 --> 00:41:18,220
they were all men.

708
00:41:18,320 --> 00:41:21,360
Despite the difficulties, in 1980,

709
00:41:21,460 --> 00:41:24,060
62 women finished their senior year,

710
00:41:24,160 --> 00:41:27,880
becoming the first women to graduate from West Point.

711
00:41:27,980 --> 00:41:32,180
The curriculum would also change to keep pace with the times.

712
00:41:32,280 --> 00:41:37,480
The period between the 70s and the early 80s

713
00:41:37,481 --> 00:41:42,480
was a period of time of increasing the number of electives

714
00:41:42,580 --> 00:41:47,580
and beginning to see those electives being grouped

715
00:41:47,680 --> 00:41:51,320
in areas of concentration that had some coherence to them.

716
00:41:51,420 --> 00:41:53,320
We still didn't have any majors.

717
00:41:53,420 --> 00:41:56,440
It wasn't until classes in the early 80s

718
00:41:56,540 --> 00:41:58,640
where we saw what we would consider

719
00:41:58,740 --> 00:42:01,740
a traditional undergraduate major.

720
00:42:01,840 --> 00:42:03,640
Though science and engineering classes

721
00:42:03,740 --> 00:42:06,140
were still prerequisites for all cadets,

722
00:42:06,141 --> 00:42:11,440
By 1985, the school offered 21 majors in many non-military

723
00:42:11,540 --> 00:42:16,580
subjects, such as behavioral science and literature.

724
00:42:16,680 --> 00:42:19,280
It's important that the curriculum at West Point

725
00:42:19,380 --> 00:42:23,000
evolve to keep pace with changing army needs and to

726
00:42:23,100 --> 00:42:24,900
keep pace with the times.

727
00:42:25,000 --> 00:42:28,200
Our officers must be prepared to lead high tech and globally

728
00:42:28,300 --> 00:42:30,700
engaged army.

729
00:42:30,800 --> 00:42:34,300
Duty, honor, country.

730
00:42:34,301 --> 00:42:38,940
West Point's motto encapsulates the essence of the school.

731
00:42:39,040 --> 00:42:42,300
Those who have passed through the halls of this institution

732
00:42:42,400 --> 00:42:46,480
have continually changed the world in which we live.

733
00:42:46,580 --> 00:42:48,980
So if you're interested in your complete development

734
00:42:49,080 --> 00:42:51,860
as a person with an emphasis also

735
00:42:51,960 --> 00:42:55,280
on honorable behavior, on ethical treatment of others,

736
00:42:55,380 --> 00:43:00,760
this is an ideal environment to hone those skills.

737
00:43:00,860 --> 00:43:02,660
West Point's goal has always been

738
00:43:02,661 --> 00:43:06,060
been to supply an intelligent, well-educated corps of officers

739
00:43:06,160 --> 00:43:09,560
to the United States Army.

740
00:43:09,660 --> 00:43:13,100
We're not educating engineers and philosophers and

741
00:43:13,200 --> 00:43:17,200
psychologists and mathematicians and scientists.

742
00:43:17,300 --> 00:43:21,720
We are using engineering and philosophy and psychology and

743
00:43:21,820 --> 00:43:25,420
mathematics and physics and chemistry to educate Army

744
00:43:25,520 --> 00:43:28,160
officers. Though the purpose has

745
00:43:28,260 --> 00:43:32,160
remained the same, the lessons have changed.

746
00:43:32,161 --> 00:43:34,200
Every battle or scientific achievement

747
00:43:34,300 --> 00:43:36,160
provides military knowledge, which

748
00:43:36,260 --> 00:43:39,700
must be handed down to the next generation of leaders.

749
00:43:39,800 --> 00:43:43,500
Charles D. Hall.

750
00:43:43,600 --> 00:43:47,700
Joseph M. From revolution to innovation,

751
00:43:47,800 --> 00:43:51,320
fortress to classroom, West Point

752
00:43:51,420 --> 00:43:54,920
has grown with the country, producing some of the nation's

753
00:43:55,020 --> 00:43:58,960
greatest leaders by teaching the ancient art of war

754
00:43:58,961 --> 00:44:00,960
in an ever-changing world.

